[agent:result]     Read the feature group into a dataframe.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Reads the feature group by default from the offline storage as Spark DataFrame on Hopsworks and Databricks, and as Pandas dataframe on AWS Sagemaker and pure Python environments.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Set `online` to `True` to read from the online storage, or change `dataframe_type` to read as a different format.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Example: Reading feature group as of latest state
[agent:result]         ```python
[agent:result]         # connect to the Feature Store
[agent:result]         fs = ...
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]         # get the Feature Group instance
[agent:result]         fg = fs.get_or_create_feature_group(...)
[agent:result]         fg.read()
[agent:result]         ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Example: Reading feature group as of specific point in time:
[agent:result]         ```python
[agent:result]         fg = fs.get_or_create_feature_group(...)
[agent:result]         fg.read("2020-10-20 07:34:11")
[agent:result]         ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Example: Reading feature group with time-based filtering on event_time:
[agent:result]         ```python
[agent:result]         fg = fs.get_or_create_feature_group(...)
[agent:result]         # Using strings
[agent:result]         fg.read(start_time="2024-01-01", end_time="2024-01-31")
[agent:result]         # Using datetime objects
[agent:result]         from datetime import datetime
[agent:result]         fg.read(start_time=datetime(2024, 1, 1), end_time=datetime(2024, 1, 31))
[agent:result]         # Reading data from yesterday to now
[agent:result]         from datetime import datetime, timedelta
[agent:result]         fg.read(start_time=datetime.now() - timedelta(days=1), end_time=datetime.now())
[agent:result]         ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Example: Incremental feature pipeline — let the scheduler supply the window.
[agent:result]         When the Hopsworks scheduler fires a job, it injects `HOPS_START_TIME`
[agent:result]         and `HOPS_END_TIME` env vars describing the data interval the run
[agent:result]         should process. If `start_time` / `end_time` are not passed to `read`,
[agent:result]         these env vars are used as defaults, so the same feature-pipeline
[agent:result]         code works whether launched by the scheduler, by a backfill
[agent:result]         (`Job.run(start_time=..., end_time=...)`), or manually:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]         ```python
[agent:result]         # No explicit time args — falls back to HOPS_START_TIME / HOPS_END_TIME
[agent:result]         # (scheduler-supplied) if set, otherwise reads the whole feature group.
[agent:result]         fg.read()
[agent:result]         ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Parameters:
[agent:result]         wallclock_time:
[agent:result]             If specified, retrieves feature group as of specific point in time.
[agent:result]             If not specified, returns as of most recent time.
[agent:result]             Strings should be formatted in one of the following formats `%Y-%m-%d`, `%Y-%m-%d %H`, `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M`, `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`, or `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S.%f`.
[agent:result]             Mutually exclusive with `start_time` / `end_time` (time-travel vs
[agent:result]             event-time filter are different operations). An explicit `wallclock_time`
[agent:result]             takes precedence over the scheduler-injected `HOPS_START_TIME` /
[agent:result]             `HOPS_END_TIME` env vars: when it is set, those defaults are ignored.
[agent:result]         online: If `True`, read from online feature store.
[agent:result]         dataframe_type:
[agent:result]             The type of the returned dataframe.
[agent:result]             By default, maps to Spark dataframe for the Spark Engine and Pandas dataframe for the Python engine.
[agent:result]         read_options:
[agent:result]             Additional options as key/value pairs to pass to the execution engine.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]             For spark engine: Dictionary of read options for Spark.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]             For python engine:
[agent:result]             - key `"arrow_flight_config"` to pass a dictionary of arrow flight configurations.
[agent:result]               For example: `{"arrow_flight_config": {"timeout": 900}}`.
[agent:result]             - key `"pandas_types"` and value `True` to retrieve columns as [Pandas nullable types](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/integer_na.html) rather than numpy/object(string) types (experimental).
[agent:result]         start_time:
[agent:result]             Inclusive lower bound on the `event_time` column (`event_time >= start_time`).
[agent:result]             If not provided and `wallclock_time` is also not set, defaults to the
[agent:result]             `HOPS_START_TIME` environment variable when set (scheduler-supplied
[agent:result]             data-interval start). When `wallclock_time` is set the env-var fallback is
[agent:result]             skipped — wallclock_time takes precedence. An explicit `start_time` always
[agent:result]             wins over both. If neither `start_time` nor `HOPS_START_TIME` is set, no
[agent:result]             lower bound is applied (the whole feature group is read).
[agent:result]             Can be a `datetime`, `date`, Unix timestamp (int), pandas `Timestamp`, or a string formatted as
[agent:result]             `%Y-%m-%d`, `%Y-%m-%d %H`, `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M`, `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`, `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S.%f`,
[agent:result]             or ISO-8601 UTC `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%fZ` (e.g. `2026-01-01T00:00:00.000000Z`).
[agent:result]             Scheduler-injected `HOPS_START_TIME` / `HOPS_END_TIME` use the ISO-8601 form.
[agent:result]         end_time:
